#!/bin/sh
# Refuse a push whose suite does not pass.
#
# Activate once per clone:  git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, precisely. The failure it prevents is recorded in #42: d0450ca
# was green, a header edit made `containment` fail, and the `&&` chain pushed anyway
# because the failure was in an earlier line of the same command's output. The suite
# WAS run. What was missing is anything that made the result binding. So this gate
# reads `run-all`'s exit status and nothing else — not its output, not a count, not
# a pattern in the log. Output is what got misread the first time.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN .git/hooks. A hook under .git/ is untracked: it cannot be
# reviewed in a diff, does not survive a clone, and is absent on the next machine.
# That is the same works-here-only shape as the corpus defect in ebb630a, where 14
# rows were readable on exactly one machine and every check passed there. A gate with
# that property would be a gate exactly where it is never needed.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT DO. It runs on the machine that wrote the code, so it cannot catch
# a defect that only appears elsewhere — ebb630a is that class and no local hook of
# any diligence would have caught it. #44 covers that and this does not subsume it.
#
# Bypass is `git push --no-verify`, and it is deliberately left available: a gate with
# no exit turns a bad five minutes into a fight with the tooling, and the honest record
# of a bypass is better than a gate someone deletes.

set -e

# git runs hooks from the repository root, so the suite path is relative to here.
if [ ! -f test/run-all.mjs ]; then
  echo "pre-push: test/run-all.mjs not found — refusing rather than assuming a pass." >&2
  echo "          A gate that cannot find the suite has not checked anything, and" >&2
  echo "          silently allowing here is what makes an absent guard read as a green one." >&2
  exit 1
fi

echo "pre-push: running the suite before publishing…" >&2

if node test/run-all.mjs; then
  exit 0
fi

# The suite's own output is above. This says what happened to the push, because the
# thing that failed the first time was noticing that a failure had scrolled past.
cat >&2 <<'MSG'

pre-push: REFUSED — the suite exited nonzero, so nothing was pushed.

  Fix it, or push with --no-verify if you are deliberately publishing a red tree
  (a rerun, a bisect, a branch you are handing someone). Using --no-verify is a
  decision; not noticing is what this gate exists to prevent.
MSG
exit 1
